BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gainssa-ai-safety-essentials-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261027T152857Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261109
SUMMARY:Deadline: GAIN SSA: AI Safety Essentials Fellowship (inaugural coho
 rt)
DESCRIPTION:GAIN SSA (Global AI Youth Network\, Sub-Saharan Africa) runs th
 e AI Safety Essentials Fellowship for residents of Africa considering how 
 they can be effective contributors in shaping the continent's transition t
 oward containing risks from transformative AI. Fellows spend six weeks map
 ping the priorities and gaps that need to be filled in Africa's AI safety 
 ecosystem\, through curated pre-reading and a live group call each week (a
 bout 5 hours per week). CURRICULUM: existential risks and catastrophic vec
 tors\; frontier AI models and Africa\; defining safe and beneficial AI\; s
 ubfields and alignment architectures\; Africa's AI safety landscape and po
 licy\; and a capstone week on fellowships and career launch\, with guidanc
 e toward international pipelines such as MATS\, ERA\, BlueDot and GovAI. M
 entorship comes from international AI safety researchers\, governance fell
 ows and peer builders. FUNDING: after the program\, top capstone action-pl
 an proposals are funded from a USD 500 to 1\,000 micro-grant pool (capped 
 at USD 1\,000)\, including proposals needing compute\, travel to AI alignm
 ent conferences in Sub-Saharan Africa\, or communication work (for example
  Substack Pro for writers)\; results are shared within 10 days of receivin
 g a proposal. The main aim is to have participants take action to build Af
 rica's first AI safety systems. APPLICATION: expression of interest for th
 e inaugural cohort via the Google Form linked from the program page. DEADL
 INE: expressions of interest close 8 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: GAIN 
 SSA (Global AI Youth Network\, Sub-Saharan Africa)\nLocation: Online (virt
 ual\, facilitated). Open to residents of African countries.\nAward: Micro-
 grant pool funding top capstone proposals at USD 500 to 1\,000 each (cappe
 d at USD 1\,000)\, usable for compute access\, communication work such as 
 Substack Pro subscriptions for writers\, or travel to AI alignment confere
 nces in Sub-Saharan Africa. The fellowship itself is free to attend (no pa
 rticipation fee\, no application fee)\; no stipend is paid during the prog
 ram.\nDuration: 6-week facilitated virtual curriculum plus an onboarding p
 re-requisites module (the site also describes it as 7 weeks)\, about 5 hou
 rs per week with one live group call per week\; capstone proposals are fun
 ded afterwards\, with results within 10 days of submission.\n\nMore: https
 ://gainssa.org/initiatives/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://gainssa.org/initiatives/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online (virtual\, facilitated). Open to residents of African count
 ries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T152857Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cambridge-africa-alborada-research-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261027T152857Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260903
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cambridge-Africa: ALBORADA Research Fund 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Cambridge-Africa ALBORADA Research Fund competitively award
 s grants to pairs of researchers\, one from an African university or resea
 rch institution and one from the University of Cambridge or an allied rese
 arch institute\, across all disciplines\, to initiate and/or strengthen re
 search collaborations. ELIGIBILITY: both applicants must be at post-doctor
 al level or above\, with employment contracts extending beyond the award e
 nd date\, and apply with the support of their Head of Department or equiva
 lent\; the Cambridge applicant must work at the University of Cambridge or
  an affiliated institute (Wellcome Sanger Institute\, NIAB\, British Antar
 ctic Survey)\; the African applicant must be based at an African universit
 y or equivalent. Limited student support is considered where it enhances t
 he Cambridge-Africa relationship. FUNDING: GBP 1\,000 to 25\,000\, coverin
 g research costs including reagents\, fieldwork\, equipment and research-t
 raining activities in Africa. APPLY: the Cambridge-based applicant registe
 rs using an institutional email (cam.ac.uk\, sanger.ac.uk\, babraham.ac.uk
 \, bas.ac.uk or niab.com) and invites the Africa-based applicant via the o
 nline form. Deadline: 3 September 2026. Queries: alboradafund@cambridge-af
 rica.cam.ac.uk.\n\nOrganisation: Cambridge-Africa Programme\, University o
 f Cambridge (supported by The ALBORADA Trust)\nLocation: Pairs of research
 ers: one at the University of Cambridge or an allied institute (Wellcome S
 anger Institute\, NIAB\, British Antarctic Survey)\, one at an African uni
 versity or research institution\nAward: Competitive grants of GBP 1\,000 t
 o 25\,000. Covers research costs such as reagents\, fieldwork\, equipment 
 and research-training activities in Africa (e.g. setting up courses/worksh
 ops). No application fee.\nDuration: Short collaborative research projects
 \n\nMore: https://www.cambridge-africa.cam.ac.uk/initiatives/2026-call-for
 -applications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.cambridge-africa.cam.ac.uk/initiatives/2026-call-for-applic
 ations?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Pairs of researchers: one at the University of Cambridge or an all
 ied institute (Wellcome Sanger Institute\, NIAB\, British Antarctic Survey
 )\, one at an African university or research institution
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:goethe-institut-aep-mobility-grants-2026-q2@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T152857Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261215
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261216
SUMMARY:Deadline: Goethe-Institut: Africa-Europe Mobility Grants (15 Decemb
 er 2026 Cut-Off)
DESCRIPTION:Sub-Saharan Component (Connect & Create) of the Africa-Europe P
 artnerships for Culture programme\, an EUR 8 million 42-month initiative (
 2025-2028) implemented by the Goethe-Institut in partnership with Expertis
 e France and Institut francais. PRIMARY FOCUS: transcontinental Africa-Afr
 ica short-term mobility for artists and culture professionals. European ar
 tists and organisations may also apply\, provided they have an existing pa
 rtnership with an African artist\, institution or organisation. ACTIVITIES
 : artistic and professional development (research\, co-creation/co-develop
 ment\, non-formal learning\, building international professional relations
 hips)\; cultural exchange and networking (residencies\, exhibitions\, conf
 erences\, workshops\, training\, collaborative international projects). Mo
 bility may be physical\, digital/virtual or hybrid. ELIGIBLE COUNTRIES: al
 l Sub-Saharan Africa countries and all EU Member States. DISCIPLINES: visu
 al arts\, performing arts\, music\, literature\, film\, media arts\, cultu
 ral heritage\, design\, architecture\, fashion design. SCHEME TOTALS: up t
 o 195 grants across the programme period\; rolling intake with quarterly c
 ut-offs. DEADLINE: this card is for the 15 June 2026 cut-off (other cut-of
 fs each have their own card). APPLY via the Goethe-Institut Africa-Europe 
 Partnerships page.\n\nOrganisation: Goethe-Institut (with Expertise France
  and Institut francais)\nLocation: Mobility within Sub-Saharan Africa (pri
 mary focus) and between Sub-Saharan Africa and Europe\nAward: Up to EUR 4\
 ,000 per mobility. Eligible costs: travel (including visa and insurance)\,
  accommodation\, subsistence\, digital costs\, and top-up support for fami
 ly\, disability\, and green mobility. No application fee.\nDuration: Rolli
 ng call with quarterly cut-offs: 15 December 2026\, 15 March 2027\, 15 Jun
 e 2027 and a final cut-off on 15 September 2027. Mobilities for the Decemb
 er cut-off take place between 1 January and 31 August 2027.\n\nMore: https
 ://www.goethe.de/prj/aep/en/cal/ssa/mga.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.goethe.de/prj/aep/en/cal/ssa/mga.html?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Mobility within Sub-Saharan Africa (primary focus) and between Sub
 -Saharan Africa and Europe
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
